# Proof: FRFT Can Be Computed in O(N log N), Same as FFT

## Answer

**Yes.** The fractional Fourier transform (FRFT) of a length-$N$ sequence can be computed in $O(N \log N)$ time, matching the computational complexity of the FFT.

---

## 1. Definition of the FRFT

The fractional Fourier transform of order $\alpha$ (a continuous parameter) of a signal $f(x)$ is defined by the integral kernel:

$$\mathcal{F}_\alpha[f](u) = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} f(x)\, K_\alpha(x, u)\, dx$$

where

$$K_\alpha(x, u) = \sqrt{\frac{1 - i\cot\alpha}{2\pi}}\; \exp\!\left(\frac{i}{2}\bigl[(x^2 + u^2)\cot\alpha - 2xu\csc\alpha\bigr]\right).$$

When $\alpha = \pi/2$, $\cot\alpha = 0$ and $\csc\alpha = 1$, so the kernel reduces to $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}} e^{-ixu}$, recovering the ordinary Fourier transform. The FRFT thus interpolates between the identity ($\alpha = 0$) and the Fourier transform ($\alpha = \pi/2$), and beyond.

## 2. Chirp Decomposition

The key algebraic observation is that the kernel factorizes. Expanding the exponent:

$$K_\alpha(x,u) = C_\alpha\; \underbrace{\exp\!\left(\frac{i}{2}u^2\cot\alpha\right)}_{\text{output chirp}}\; \underbrace{\exp\!\left(\frac{i}{2}x^2\cot\alpha\right)}_{\text{input chirp}}\; \underbrace{\exp\!\left(-\frac{i\,xu}{\sin\alpha}\right)}_{\text{Fourier kernel}},$$

where $C_\alpha = \sqrt{\frac{1-i\cot\alpha}{2\pi}}$ is a constant depending only on $\alpha$.

Therefore the FRFT can be written as a **three-step procedure**:

$$\mathcal{F}_\alpha[f](u) = C_\alpha\; \exp\!\left(\frac{i}{2}u^2\cot\alpha\right) \cdot \underbrace{\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \left[\exp\!\left(\frac{i}{2}x^2\cot\alpha\right) f(x)\right] \exp\!\left(-i\,x\,\frac{u}{\sin\alpha}\right) dx}_{\text{ordinary Fourier transform of } g(x) = e^{ix^2\cot\alpha/2}\,f(x), \text{ evaluated at } \omega = u/\sin\alpha}$$

Denoting $g(x) = \exp\!\bigl(\frac{i}{2}x^2\cot\alpha\bigr)\, f(x)$, the integral is precisely $\hat{g}(\omega)$ with $\omega = u/\sin\alpha$, where $\hat{g}$ is the standard Fourier transform.

**Step 1 (Input chirp multiplication):** Compute $g(x) = \exp\!\bigl(\frac{i}{2}x^2\cot\alpha\bigr)\, f(x)$.

**Step 2 (Fourier transform):** Compute $\hat{g}(\omega)$, the ordinary Fourier transform of $g$.

**Step 3 (Output chirp multiplication + scaling):** Compute $\mathcal{F}_\alpha[f](u) = C_\alpha\, \exp\!\bigl(\frac{i}{2}u^2\cot\alpha\bigr)\, \hat{g}(u/\sin\alpha)$.

## 3. Complexity Analysis (Continuous → Discrete)

In the discrete setting, we work with $N$ samples. The approach of Ozaktas, Arikan, et al. (1996) discretizes the above procedure:

- **Step 1:** Multiply each of the $N$ samples by a precomputed chirp value $\exp\!\bigl(\frac{i}{2}x_k^2\cot\alpha\bigr)$.  
  **Cost:** $O(N)$.

- **Step 2:** Compute the FFT of the resulting $N$-length sequence.  
  **Cost:** $O(N \log N)$.

- **Step 3:** Multiply each output sample by a precomputed chirp value $\exp\!\bigl(\frac{i}{2}u_k^2\cot\alpha\bigr)$ and the constant $C_\alpha$, with appropriate rescaling of the frequency axis by $1/\sin\alpha$.  
  **Cost:** $O(N)$.

**Total cost:**

$$O(N) + O(N \log N) + O(N) = O(N \log N).$$

## 4. Equivalence to FFT Complexity

The standard FFT computes the discrete Fourier transform of a length-$N$ sequence in $\Theta(N \log N)$ operations (Cooley–Tukey). The FRFT computation described above requires:

- Two $O(N)$ chirp multiplications,
- One $O(N \log N)$ FFT call,

so its asymptotic complexity is $\Theta(N \log N)$ — **identical** to the FFT up to constant factors.

The constant factor is modest: the FRFT requires roughly $2\times$ the work of a single FFT (one forward FFT plus two linear-time passes), so it is practical as well as asymptotically optimal.

## 5. Why $O(N \log N)$ Is Optimal

The FRFT is a unitary linear operator on an $N$-dimensional space, so a generic $N \times N$ matrix–vector product costs $O(N^2)$. The $O(N \log N)$ bound is achievable precisely because the FRFT kernel has **chirp structure**: it factorizes into chirp multiplications and an ordinary Fourier transform. Since the FFT itself achieves the information-theoretic lower bound of $\Omega(N \log N)$ for computing the DFT (which is a special case of the FRFT at $\alpha = \pi/2$), no algorithm for the general FRFT can do better than $O(N \log N)$ in general. Thus the chirp-based algorithm is **asymptotically optimal**.

## 6. Conclusion

The fractional Fourier transform can be decomposed into two chirp multiplications and one ordinary Fourier transform. Since chirp multiplication is $O(N)$ and the Fourier transform can be computed by the FFT in $O(N \log N)$, the total computational complexity of the FRFT is $O(N \log N)$ — the same as the FFT. This bound is also optimal, since the FRFT subsumes the DFT as a special case.

### PROOF COMPLETE
